WEBVTT

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.730
<i>The year is 1889.</i>

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.900
<i>The Nautilus sets her course for her secret base at the South Pole</i>

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.940
<i>in order to replenish her repair and maintenance supplies.</i>

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.650
<i>Nadia and the Grandis trio</i>

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.360
<i>are anxious about arriving on the unknown Antarctic Continent.</i>

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.280
<i>But Jean is overjoyed at the prospect.</i>

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.660
<i>On the way, the Nautilus is subjected to nature's fury</i>

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.370
<i>by an undersea volcano,</i>

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.290
<i>and an attack by a gigantic nautilus shellfish.</i>

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:51.710
<i>The Nautilus finally surfaces in a huge cavern beneath Antarctica.</i>

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:17.880
Finally!

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.180
Gee, what a fantastic place!

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:32.770
I never knew such a modern facility existed at the South Pole.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.520
Look, Nadia!

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.190
It's so hot!

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.820
Who said that the South Pole was ice cold?

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:45.490
Geez, it's hot! It's hot!
It's hot! It's hot! It's hot!

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.910
Pervert!

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.920
Geez, Jean, you lied to me!

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.590
This is strange. The South Pole is supposed to be very cold!

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.760
That's correct.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.590
Even that arctic clothing wouldn't be enough to protect you.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.640
But that's on the surface way up above.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.350
We are deep within a subterranean strata,

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.850
just above pools of molten lava.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.110
That's why it's so warm here.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.610
This may be an ice-covered continent unexplored by man.

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.860
But tens of thousands of years ago,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.450
the people of Atlantis had already built a modern base on the shores

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.450
of an underground lake deep within the bowels of this continent.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.540
Miss Electra...

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:39.630
You want to say you would like to see this marvelous base, right?

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.090
Yes, can I?

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.180
You'd go exploring even if I said no.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.680
Yes, you can go.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.390
Really? Oh, boy!

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.930
So, this is the supply base?

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.310
Is that hill facing us mined as a source for your materials?

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:02.690
Right. Over there, we have iron ore, bauxite, and manganese ore.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.900
This place is a treasure house.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:19.210
Say, Eiko, what are those three pipes and the tank?

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:26.130
Oh, that's oil. We're underground, so the crude oil flows downward.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.510
Fantastic!

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.640
With all this oil, we'd be filthy rich!

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.600
But how will you take it back? Only the Nautilus could do that.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.810
That's why I hate men.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.190
All they think about is money.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.820
This is the bridge calling the engine room.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.280
Please report on the status of repairs.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.990
This is the main engine room.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.000
We'll need another fifteen hours to complete our repairs.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.580
Roger.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:57.960
First Officer.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:58.460
Yes, Sir?

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.840
I'm going ashore for awhile.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.090
Have all repairs completed within sixteen hours

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.170
and be ready to sail at o-seven hundred tomorrow morning.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:07.470
Aye, Sir!

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.680
Carry on.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.010
Yes, Sir.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:13.220
He's going to that place.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:18.230
All hands, hear this! All work must be completed within 16 hours.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:21.520
We'll be shipping out tomorrow morning at o-seven hundred hours!

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:30.740
Now it's Marie's turn.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.500
Wow!

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.670
This is the way a man should work.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:52.720
It's hard to tell which one is the kid.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:58.270
Jean, Nadia, get your arctic clothing and come with me.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:06:59.730
Yes, Sir!

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:02.940
What's the matter, Nadia?

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.940
Is that an order?

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:06.690
That's right.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.570
I understand.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:09.900
Hurry.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.370
Captain, I'm going also!

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:14.530
Marie wants to go too!

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.950
Sorry, not this time.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.340
Fantastic! Are we going up in this?

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.420
Yes.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:38.140
Gosh, isn't this amazing, Nadia?
That's ice outside.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.600
We're passing through a tunnel of ice.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.110
What was that? It looked like a tree branch.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.190
That's what it was.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.440
That is the world tree.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:51.490
World tree?

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:53.610
Yes, the tree that holds up the skies,

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.410
whose roots spread all over this globe.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:08:00.000
It is a tree of incredible size, said to be the source of all life.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.920
Finding a tree imbedded in ice here in the Antarctic region

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.960
seems to indicate that it was warm here at one time.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.590
You can't be serious, Nadia!

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:14.390
How can a gigantic continent like this move from place to place?

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:16.390
No, I'm afraid she's right.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:21.350
It is man's vanity to assume that continents cannot move.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:27.440
Jean, can you tell me what is most important to a scientist?

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:29.400
It's a sixth sense.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:30.740
Sixth sense?

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.400
That's right, Jean.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.030
Do you remember what the world map looks like?

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:35.490
Yes, Sir.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.370
Then think of the coastal outline of the two American continents

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.950
and those of Europe and Africa.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.880
Can you see how the lines fit against each other?

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:48.750
I see what you mean.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:51.710
They fit perfectly!

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:55.930
But I still find it hard to believe that continents can move.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.510
With the technology available in the 19th century,

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:02.140
it is impossible to prove such movements.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.690
But there will come a time when science will find definite proof.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:13.690
The seed of all inventions and discoveries ever made can be found

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.280
in the sixth sense of the inventor and his need to prove it.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.120
That's science.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:20.410
I see!

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:33.760
Thank you for the hard work.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.720
Where's the Captain?

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.180
He went out, taking Jean and Nadia with him.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.640
What? To that place?

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.100
I'm quite sure that's where he took them.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.190
I guess he felt he had to.

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.940
This may be the last time.

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.030
Gosh, this is fantastic!

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.080
The compass needle won't stay still.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.410
What does that mean?

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:08.750
On this spot, the magnetic forces of the Earth are all mixed up.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.880
Jean, Nadia, come on. Over here.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.960
Yes, Sir! Sorry!

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.970
What is this?

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:19.090
A moving sidewalk.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.850
They look like a row of round bars laid crosswise.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:23.260
Look closer.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.640
This is amazing! They're floating on air!

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.060
That's right. They've lightened the pull of gravity,

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.070
and utilized magnetic forces to move it forward.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:36.740
A terrific invention! Who made it?

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:41.200
The people of ancient Atlantis, 20,000 years ago.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.830
It is a relic of a lost civilization.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.500
Now, get on.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:48.040
Yes, Sir.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:01.720
Unbelievable! I wonder how the effects of gravity are decreased?

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:03.970
What is it?

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.180
There's something there!

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.140
Over there!

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:08.730
What are those?

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.440
Prehistoric animals?

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.650
They look like angels.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:15.020
They look more like devils to me.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.110
Captain Nemo, what are those?

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.820
This place is a museum made of ice.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:20.910
Museum?

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.200
That's right.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.370
Species of animals that disappeared in the distant past are kept here.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:29.460
A while ago, I saw something that looked like dragons with spears.

00:11:29.830 --> 00:11:31.670
Those were homodinasonics.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:38.260
They are saurians. They ruled the Earth at one time.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:41.050
Saurians had an uncanny ability

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:45.140
to adapt their bodies swiftly to changes in environment.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.890
They evolved at a fast pace,

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:52.650
and established a civilization on Earth before mankind.

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:55.150
They were the first inhabitants of this planet.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:01.070
However, seventy million years ago they fell victim to cosmic rays

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:05.280
created by black spots on the sun, and vanished from the Earth.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.330
We are not the only ones endowed with intelligence.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.450
We must never forget that.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:11.500
Yes, Sir.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.250
Life is a mysterious thing.

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:16.960
It is.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.970
Wow, it's big!

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:26.510
I wonder what's inside?

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:32.430
What's that?

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.770
Penguins!

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:41.690
What?

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:50.410
Incredible!

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.370
It's like an ice palace!

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:57.330
This is it.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.710
What's inside here?

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:01.260
We'll know when we enter.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:44.170
Jean, what is that?

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:44.970
I don't know!

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:51.430
It's been a long while.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.640
What? Jean, what's the matter with you?

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.310
Hello, old friend, it's good to see you again.

00:13:58.310 --> 00:13:59.440
Who's that?

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:11.120
What are you? What's happening? Who's speaking?

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:15.870
Captain Nemo?

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:22.750
I see.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.630
Jean, Nadia, both of you come over here.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:29.890
Yes, Sir.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:36.810
Let me introduce Nadia.
Sorry, I mean Nadia and Jean.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:41.230
And this is my good friend Irion.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:42.940
This whale is your friend?

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.530
Mr. Whale?

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.030
Yes, that's his name.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:52.370
Captain Nemo, you're amazing, having a whale for a friend!

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.530
Just like Nadia.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.540
He wants to talk to you.

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:57.790
Talk to us?

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:00.210
Yes. I'll wait outside.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:01.630
But...

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.290
There's nothing to be afraid of.

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:05.840
But...

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.930
I see, you are Nadia.

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:17.390
I can't believe it!

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.850
Are you really talking?

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:24.820
That's right. I'm speaking directly into your heart.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:26.020
My heart?

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:30.990
That's right. It's simple when we use the Blue Water.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.570
The Blue Water?

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:34.910
That's right.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:41.080
You don't have to actually talk. Just think what you want to say.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:44.590
I'll try. Don't you have something to say to Captain Nemo?

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:47.050
We've already exchanged our farewells.

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:48.510
Farewells?

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:55.180
Yes, we won't be seeing each other again on this Earth.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:56.640
Why not?

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:03.060
I have lived too long. My life will be over soon.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:05.610
Long? How long?

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:12.650
During my lifetime, the Earth has circled the sun 20,000 times.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:14.360
20,000 years?

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:19.240
Yes, according to the human calendar.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:22.370
During those many years, you observed the history of mankind?

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:27.420
Yes, man is a most interesting species of life.

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.510
Mankind initiates wars in seeking peace,

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.800
and wages wars to destroy peace.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.930
Yes, they are a most interesting species of life.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:38.760
That can't be.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.520
But that is the truth.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.660
What was that?

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.990
I just sent you some images from my memories.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:04.330
I'm afraid we are what you say we are.

00:17:04.330 --> 00:17:05.120
However, Nadia,

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:11.590
mankind is an interesting species of life, but not foolish.

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:14.050
That is my belief.

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:17.590
Nemo is my last friend.

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:22.600
As his final gesture, he introduced you two to me.

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:24.730
For that I am deeply grateful.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:30.150
He believes in you, and has entrusted the future to you.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:37.070
Please honor his trust in you.
That is my final wish.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:41.870
To meet and become friends is a most precious thing.

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:46.080
As my final gift to you, I will tell you what you are seeking.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.000
The person you seek is very close to you.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:56.430
Also, you will meet another, most unexpected person.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.850
An unexpected person?

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:00.890
My brother?

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:06.810
It's true, isn't it? I do have a brother!

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.280
I feel I'll have a wonderful dream tonight.

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:19.280
Thank you, my children.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:25.290
Farewell, inheritors of the Blue Water.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.080
Wait! There are other things I want to know!

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:32.040
Nadia, what's wrong? You look dazed.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:36.510
The Blue Water started shining, and then became dark again right away.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:37.800
Right away?

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:40.760
Yes, in about three seconds.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:44.220
I see. Was that all a dream?

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.270
Jean, let's go.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:48.190
All right.

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:55.940
Hello!

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:57.650
Can you understand what it's saying?

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.530
I guess so. But such a thing has never happened before!

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.290
It's saying we should go that way.

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.460
Maybe it wants to take us where Captain Nemo is.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.460
It seems that way!

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:12.960
Let's go.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:13.380
Yes.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:16.550
This is amazing!

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.920
You talked with the whale!

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:20.720
Yes.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:24.550
You were the only one that could talk to the whale and penguin.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:25.720
I wonder why?

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:30.440
The whale was saying it was because of the Blue Water.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.730
And what did you talk about?

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:38.940
Sorry, can I tell you later when I'm not so confused?

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:44.450
Hello, guys. Do you know where Captain Nemo is?

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:46.740
How should we know?

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:48.500
What did you say?

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:51.330
I'm sorry! I'm afraid I don't know.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.790
I see. And the rest of you?

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:57.170
We've taken on a full supply of foodstuffs,

00:19:57.170 --> 00:19:59.920
but I guess all you want is soup, right?

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:02.220
We'll go and find him right away!

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.220
Children should always be obedient!

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:07.720
She's a fearsome woman.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:11.520
Oh, my precious Nemo, where have you gone?

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:12.940
We're going to the surface.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:15.400
You mean the Antarctic Continent?

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:16.610
That's right.

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:18.150
Oh, boy!

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.580
Gosh, it's so wide!

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:36.210
It's so cold!

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.420
It's become night all of a sudden.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:39.500
Jean.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.590
Yes, Sir?

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.630
This is the South Pole.

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:45.470
Right here?

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:46.600
That's right.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:51.310
All right! This is incredible! I'm standing over the South Pole!

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:54.060
I'm overwhelmed!

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.480
But actually you were just brought here by Captain Nemo.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:20:59.650
That's true.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:02.360
I didn't come here on my own.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:03.820
No, you didn't.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:09.830
We are standing here thanks to the Nautilus and the Atlanteans.

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:13.500
No man has set foot here before.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.880
But in the 20th century mankind will break through the ice,

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.210
and reach this point under their own power.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.840
I put my faith in the power of mankind.

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:37.270
What's that?

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:38.770
An aurora.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:39.480
What is that?

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:41.150
It's beautiful!

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:44.190
In scientific terms, electrons and protons from the sun

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:49.240
strike the Earth's atmosphere and create this phenomena.

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:55.580
Such theories can never truly describe the beauty of nature.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.420
A tear?

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:02.630
It can't be!

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:07.760
He believes in you, and has entrusted the future to you.

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:19.690
Okay, it's ready!

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:21.270
What is this?

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:22.610
Ice cream.

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:23.900
Ice cream?

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:27.860
Yes, I made it in my fully automatic ice cream maker.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:29.990
When did you do that?

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:32.330
I made it especially for you, Marie.

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:34.080
Go ahead, try it.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:39.920
My head hurts!

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:43.300
But it's good!

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:45.710
Well, how is it?

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:49.590
I never knew ice cream was so delicious.

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:53.390
This ice cream was made from South Pole ice.

00:22:53.390 --> 00:22:54.140
The South Pole?

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.390
The ice here is supposed to be 12,000 years old.

00:22:57.390 --> 00:23:00.350
Then it's like we're eating the history of our world.

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:03.110
I didn't know the world was so small!

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:08.900
12,000 year-old ice cream...

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:13.160
Compared to that, a man's life is truly brief.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:17.450
The important thing is how we live that brief interval.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:24.040
What's the matter with you three?

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:26.960
We went to the surface looking for Captain Nemo,

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.220
and nearly froze to death!

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:31.260
You went out in that clothing?

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:34.550
Well, it was so warm down here.

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:36.810
I've seen enough ice to last me a lifetime!

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:40.190
No fun!
